1
00:00:03,580 --> 00:00:05,760
either. Okay, so neck pain.

2
00:00:06,320 --> 00:00:08,220
Your last lecture
in the lower back

3
00:00:08,220 --> 00:00:10,380
covered some general
spine concepts, so

4
00:00:10,380 --> 00:00:11,780
there's no need to
repeat them here.

5
00:00:12,020 --> 00:00:14,220
Mostly I'm going to focus
on cervical specific

6
00:00:14,220 --> 00:00:16,680
issues and make comparisons
with the lumbar.

7
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:18,940
If you look at most
of the literature,

8
00:00:18,940 --> 00:00:20,160
you'll see that
most of the work

9
00:00:20,160 --> 00:00:22,220
done on the spine
is for the lumbar.

10
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:25,020
Now, the cervical spine
relative to the lumbar

11
00:00:25,020 --> 00:00:27,880
spine is kind of like
pediatrics relative to internal

12
00:00:27,880 --> 00:00:31,160
medicine. Kids aren't
miniature adults and the

13
00:00:31,160 --> 00:00:34,280
cervical spine isn't the
lumbar spine higher up.

14
00:00:34,880 --> 00:00:37,120
But that's exactly how
it's been treated and

15
00:00:37,120 --> 00:00:40,480
a lot of the work on lumbar
was just extrapolated

16
00:00:40,480 --> 00:00:42,960
onto the c-spine and
assumed to be valid.

17
00:00:43,080 --> 00:00:45,060
For our purposes today,

18
00:00:45,220 --> 00:00:47,200
mostly that's good
enough, but you should be

19
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:49,060
aware that we do know
less about the cervical

20
00:00:49,060 --> 00:00:51,320
spine with respect to
mechanisms of injury

21
00:00:51,320 --> 00:00:53,960
and disease as well as
effective treatment.

22
00:00:55,900 --> 00:00:59,340
This lecture is about
neck pain, that is axial

23
00:00:59,340 --> 00:01:02,320
pain. Axial meaning
the trunk, head, neck,

24
00:01:02,320 --> 00:01:04,660
anything around the
long axis of the body.

25
00:01:04,820 --> 00:01:07,780
In MRI, when you get these
slices from superior to

26
00:01:07,780 --> 00:01:10,640
inferior, in basic sciences
we call those transverse

27
00:01:10,640 --> 00:01:13,900
planes, but commonly they're
referred to as axial views.

28
00:01:15,300 --> 00:01:17,420
So that's what we're
going to focus on

29
00:01:17,420 --> 00:01:19,220
here, but I have to
talk a little bit about

30
00:01:19,220 --> 00:01:21,080
nerve root and
radicular involvement.

31
00:01:21,600 --> 00:01:25,440
Again contrasting it to
the lumbar spine, here

32
00:01:25,440 --> 00:01:28,420
you have nerve roots exiting
above their respective

33
00:01:28,480 --> 00:01:30,840
vertebrae because of
this eighth cervical

34
00:01:30,840 --> 00:01:33,200
nerve root which I'm sure
you already know about.

35
00:01:35,160 --> 00:01:38,440
So for a central or
paracentral disc protrusion,

36
00:01:38,440 --> 00:01:41,400
the exiting root in the
lumbar is already gone

37
00:01:41,400 --> 00:01:43,100
and you're just getting
the descending nerve

38
00:01:43,100 --> 00:01:46,040
root. In the c-spine
you're catching both and

39
00:01:46,040 --> 00:01:48,240
for a lateral or foraminal
you're getting the

40
00:01:48,240 --> 00:01:51,260
root associated with that
vertebrae as it exits.

41
00:01:56,130 --> 00:01:58,850
When somebody comes to you
with neck pain, even without

42
00:01:58,850 --> 00:02:00,730
pain in the arms, you
still want to do your due

43
00:02:00,730 --> 00:02:03,570
diligence and check them
neurologically. You'll often

44
00:02:03,570 --> 00:02:06,170
see this kind of upper cross
posturing, which is not

45
00:02:06,170 --> 00:02:09,310
specific for anything. This
puts pressure on some of

46
00:02:09,310 --> 00:02:12,370
the discs, some of the
facets, on some of the nerve

47
00:02:12,370 --> 00:02:15,390
root, it overburdens the
muscles. It's just one big

48
00:02:15,390 --> 00:02:18,050
common sign that's low
-hanging fruit for starting to

49
00:02:18,050 --> 00:02:20,530
get in there and correcting
things. But with respect

50
00:02:20,530 --> 00:02:23,690
to the exam, these are the
specific actions and muscles

51
00:02:23,690 --> 00:02:26,310
you test that reflect
strength at those levels

52
00:02:26,310 --> 00:02:30,830
faithfully. Elbow flexion is
for C5, wrist extension is

53
00:02:30,830 --> 00:02:35,830
C6, elbow extension is C7,
finger flexion is C8 but

54
00:02:35,830 --> 00:02:38,010
specifically profundus so
you kind of want to lock

55
00:02:38,010 --> 00:02:41,990
out the proximal interphalangeal
joint and just strength

56
00:02:41,990 --> 00:02:44,970
test flexion at the distal
interphalangeal joint.

57
00:02:45,390 --> 00:02:47,830
And then finger
abduction is C1 but

58
00:02:47,830 --> 00:02:49,950
specifically abductor
digiti minimi.

59
00:02:52,450 --> 00:02:55,550
For sensation, the dermatomes
can overlap, so the

60
00:02:55,550 --> 00:02:58,470
areas I have down here
are dependable every time.

61
00:02:58,750 --> 00:03:01,510
So the dermatomes in
general, the strength

62
00:03:01,510 --> 00:03:03,310
in general, and
definitely the reflexes

63
00:03:03,310 --> 00:03:05,090
are things that you
should just know. So

64
00:03:10,550 --> 00:03:13,750
if you suspect nerve root
involvement because they

65
00:03:13,750 --> 00:03:16,270
have pain and maybe some
numbness in the arm, a very

66
00:03:16,270 --> 00:03:19,030
good confirmation is
spiraling. The original paper

67
00:03:19,030 --> 00:03:21,370
described this as side
bending with a little axial

68
00:03:21,370 --> 00:03:25,590
compression. Now you see
every possible variation, with

69
00:03:25,590 --> 00:03:27,930
and without extension,
with and without rotation,

70
00:03:28,530 --> 00:03:31,990
whichever way you go,
go easy. If it's a

71
00:03:31,990 --> 00:03:34,510
legitimate radiculopathy,
just positioning it like

72
00:03:34,510 --> 00:03:37,070
that usually makes the
symptoms worse. Add

73
00:03:37,070 --> 00:03:39,310
compression if it doesn't,
but do it gingerly.

74
00:03:40,130 --> 00:03:44,130
So I will say that extension
closes off the foramen,

75
00:03:44,130 --> 00:03:47,290
rotating to the ipsilateral
side of symptoms closes

76
00:03:47,290 --> 00:03:50,970
the foramen and side bending
to that side closes the

77
00:03:50,970 --> 00:03:54,030
foramen. So that whenever
you see some variation or

78
00:03:54,030 --> 00:03:56,790
another on an exam, as long
as you know the mechanisms

79
00:03:56,790 --> 00:03:59,170
behind the movements, you
can kind of see through that

80
00:03:59,170 --> 00:04:01,670
particular iteration and
understand what's going on.

81
00:04:01,850 --> 00:04:03,650
And then shoulder abduction can

82
00:04:03,650 --> 00:04:05,270
often make the arm
symptoms better.

83
00:04:05,450 --> 00:04:08,150
This doesn't get much
attention in books or exams,

84
00:04:08,150 --> 00:04:09,690
but basically what's
happening is that you're

85
00:04:09,690 --> 00:04:12,270
taking traction off of the
nerve roots that are coming

86
00:04:12,270 --> 00:04:15,190
down, and laterally, by
abducting the shoulder.

87
00:04:18,650 --> 00:04:20,790
With the lumbar
spine, it was weakness

88
00:04:20,790 --> 00:04:22,630
that was the big
red flag, including

89
00:04:22,630 --> 00:04:24,830
weakness of bowel and
bladder sphincters.

90
00:04:25,010 --> 00:04:27,070
Here you get weakness,
which you can call a

91
00:04:27,070 --> 00:04:29,110
negative finding, but
that's usually accompanied

92
00:04:29,110 --> 00:04:31,590
by this positive finding
of hypertonicity.

93
00:04:32,290 --> 00:04:34,550
Anything lesioning the
upper motor neuron will

94
00:04:34,550 --> 00:04:36,990
interfere with descending
inhibition of the lower

95
00:04:36,990 --> 00:04:39,630
motor neuron, so you get
this excessive firing.

96
00:04:40,930 --> 00:04:43,500
the hand distally,
partly because there's no

97
00:04:43,500 --> 00:04:46,370
interneuron that needs to be
inhibited there. So you'll

98
00:04:46,370 --> 00:04:48,350
often get more typical
weakness, and patients

99
00:04:48,350 --> 00:04:51,670
talk about not having fine
motor control of their

100
00:04:51,670 --> 00:04:54,450
hand, difficulty with
handling buttons or writing.

101
00:04:55,010 --> 00:04:58,510
The reflexes are jumpy.
Babinski can be positive.

102
00:04:59,810 --> 00:05:02,410
Spasticity. Spasticity
is a velocity

103
00:05:02,410 --> 00:05:04,970
-dependent increase in
muscle tone, where the

104
00:05:04,970 --> 00:05:06,930
faster that you move
a joint through its

105
00:05:06,930 --> 00:05:08,930
range of motion, the
more it'll fight you.

106
00:05:09,390 --> 00:05:12,310
Clonus is similar. If
you yank the foot into

107
00:05:12,310 --> 00:05:15,490
dorsiflexion, you get this
tremor of dorsiflexion-plantar

108
00:05:15,490 --> 00:05:18,930
flexion. You can have one
or two beats of clonus,

109
00:05:18,930 --> 00:05:22,370
which suggests a more mild
version of this syndrome,

110
00:05:22,370 --> 00:05:24,550
or you can get a more
sustained version of it.

111
00:05:27,410 --> 00:05:30,910
All of this really speaks
to decreased inhibition by

112
00:05:30,910 --> 00:05:33,750
upper motor neurons. In the
legs, you generally have

113
00:05:33,750 --> 00:05:36,830
extensor tone dominating.
so with their walking it's

114
00:05:36,830 --> 00:05:39,130
hard for them to bend their
knee but distally their

115
00:05:39,130 --> 00:05:41,910
foot drags because it's
weak. This makes for a very

116
00:05:41,910 --> 00:05:44,890
functionally long leg
when you're walking and so

117
00:05:44,890 --> 00:05:47,070
trying to swing your leg
through becomes problematic

118
00:05:47,070 --> 00:05:49,350
because you can't dorsiflex
so you can't lift your

119
00:05:49,350 --> 00:05:52,110
foot off the floor when
your leg is trying to clear.

120
00:05:52,110 --> 00:05:54,590
Likewise you can't bend
your knee and so again it's

121
00:05:54,590 --> 00:05:56,990
harder to clear your leg
as you're swinging through.

122
00:05:58,210 --> 00:06:00,650
These patients
usually end up kind of

123
00:06:01,150 --> 00:06:03,750
throwing their leg out in
this cone formation. We

124
00:06:03,750 --> 00:06:06,490
call it circumduction. It's
not always super obvious,

125
00:06:06,490 --> 00:06:08,730
but once you see it a few
times, you start to get

126
00:06:08,730 --> 00:06:12,490
the consistency in patterns
from one patient to the

127
00:06:12,490 --> 00:06:15,430
next. They can also have
bowel and bladder issues.

128
00:06:16,070 --> 00:06:18,370
So I remember this one
patient that was sent to

129
00:06:18,370 --> 00:06:21,310
me from a primary care
physician for neck pain.

130
00:06:21,430 --> 00:06:24,470
And this patient comes in
with what to me was clearly

131
00:06:24,470 --> 00:06:27,070
a spastic gait, but he
had been like that for a

132
00:06:27,070 --> 00:06:29,770
while and it developed
gradually, so he sort of just

133
00:06:29,770 --> 00:06:32,950
assimilated into it. And
it wasn't terrible, but on

134
00:06:32,950 --> 00:06:35,770
top of that, he had a
sustained clonus, the reflexes

135
00:06:35,770 --> 00:06:38,690
were very brisk, and yeah,
he had spasticity. So

136
00:06:38,690 --> 00:06:42,110
basically, he had a slowly
developing spinal cord injury.

137
00:06:42,170 --> 00:06:44,370
That kind of thing needs
to get a neurosurgical

138
00:06:44,370 --> 00:06:48,810
evaluation. They talk about,
in these circles of like

139
00:06:48,810 --> 00:06:52,130
spine medicine and pain
medicine and spine surgery,

140
00:06:52,250 --> 00:06:55,630
they talk about patients
with cervical stenosis that's

141
00:06:55,630 --> 00:06:58,490
bad enough to give a
presentation like this as

142
00:06:58,490 --> 00:07:00,970
being very vulnerable
because if they fall and they

143
00:07:00,970 --> 00:07:03,130
hit their head and there's
a torque on their neck they

144
00:07:03,130 --> 00:07:05,870
could end up paralyzed.
I've never seen anything

145
00:07:05,870 --> 00:07:08,290
like that in the literature
in terms of cases. I've

146
00:07:08,290 --> 00:07:11,050
covered inpatient spinal
cord injury units for years

147
00:07:11,050 --> 00:07:13,210
and I've never seen a
patient like that but

148
00:07:13,210 --> 00:07:16,630
theoretically it's possible.
So in general you advise

149
00:07:16,630 --> 00:07:19,370
moderation of activity for
these patients and you get

150
00:07:19,370 --> 00:07:22,550
them a spine surgery
evaluation as soon as possible.

151
00:07:25,010 --> 00:07:27,590
The spinal cord is much
thicker in the cervical

152
00:07:27,590 --> 00:07:30,130
region because all those
nerves haven't peeled

153
00:07:30,130 --> 00:07:32,910
off and exited yet
and so even though the

154
00:07:32,910 --> 00:07:35,810
central foramen is bigger
it is a tight fit there.

155
00:07:37,090 --> 00:07:39,570
You can also see how
much bigger the lumbar

156
00:07:39,570 --> 00:07:44,330
vertebral body is compared
to the cervical and you

157
00:07:44,330 --> 00:07:46,390
can see it here as well
and really that just

158
00:07:46,390 --> 00:07:49,490
underscores the function of
the lumbar spine which is

159
00:07:49,490 --> 00:07:52,670
to hold upwards of 65
-70% of your body weight.

160
00:07:56,680 --> 00:07:59,110
One thing to notice over
here is the orientation

161
00:07:59,110 --> 00:08:01,130
of the facet joints.
In the lumbar they're

162
00:08:01,130 --> 00:08:03,970
sagittal so you get mostly
flexion and extension. In

163
00:08:03,970 --> 00:08:05,970
the cervical they're
midway between a coronal

164
00:08:05,970 --> 00:08:08,470
and a transverse plane.
They're about 45 degrees.

165
00:08:11,070 --> 00:08:14,310
The foramen are also
oriented 45 degrees midway

166
00:08:14,310 --> 00:08:16,870
between coronal and sagittal.
In the lumbar they're

167
00:08:16,870 --> 00:08:18,950
90 degrees from the
coronal, which is to say

168
00:08:18,950 --> 00:08:21,210
that they're sagittal,
so the spinal nerve exits

169
00:08:21,210 --> 00:08:23,770
perpendicular to your
screen. In the cervical,

170
00:08:23,770 --> 00:08:26,390
it's coming out about 45
degrees from your screen.

171
00:08:29,810 --> 00:08:32,630
So the foramen and the
transverse processes

172
00:08:32,630 --> 00:08:35,010
are 45 degrees, and
the facet joints.

173
00:08:35,270 --> 00:08:37,430
Imagine that the facet
joints were purely

174
00:08:37,430 --> 00:08:40,010
horizontal or on a
transverse plane. Then

175
00:08:40,010 --> 00:08:41,830
movement there would
give you pure rotation.

176
00:08:42,010 --> 00:08:44,890
Now imagine they were
purely coronal. Movement

177
00:08:44,890 --> 00:08:47,530
there would give you pure
side bending. So when

178
00:08:47,530 --> 00:08:49,590
they tell you that side
bending and rotation are

179
00:08:49,590 --> 00:08:51,970
in the same direction,
like in type 2 somatic

180
00:08:51,970 --> 00:08:54,230
dysfunctions, it's not so
much that they're separate

181
00:08:54,230 --> 00:08:56,410
movements, it's a
hybrid movement. They're

182
00:08:56,410 --> 00:08:59,010
rotating on this oblique
plane, so side bending

183
00:08:59,010 --> 00:09:01,390
and rotation are kind of
baked into each other.

184
00:09:04,670 --> 00:09:07,590
Another thing that sets
cervical vertebrae apart is

185
00:09:07,590 --> 00:09:10,290
the concavity on the top
of the body. The lateral

186
00:09:10,290 --> 00:09:13,790
ends have these processes
that, when you stack

187
00:09:13,790 --> 00:09:16,730
them, make a sort of joint
with the inferior body of

188
00:09:16,730 --> 00:09:19,590
the vertebrae above it.
So these unco-vertebral

189
00:09:19,590 --> 00:09:21,790
articulations act like a
kind of joint and they can

190
00:09:21,790 --> 00:09:24,410
develop hypertrophic changes
with arthropathy just

191
00:09:24,410 --> 00:09:27,370
like facet joints that can
contribute to pathology.

192
00:09:30,470 --> 00:09:33,570
Normal AP view. You can
see the anatomy nicely

193
00:09:33,570 --> 00:09:36,090
here especially the unco
-vertebral articulations.

194
00:09:38,350 --> 00:09:41,170
Normal lateral view.
You can't see the

195
00:09:41,170 --> 00:09:43,170
foramen clearly because
they're 45 degrees

196
00:09:43,170 --> 00:09:44,950
so they're kind of
hiding behind the body.

197
00:09:47,170 --> 00:09:48,370
For comparison,

198
00:09:48,490 --> 00:09:50,550
the transverse process
with the foramen is in

199
00:09:50,550 --> 00:09:53,220
green, the blue are the
articular pillars with the

200
00:09:53,220 --> 00:09:55,650
facet joints that you
palpate all the time in lab.

201
00:09:59,590 --> 00:10:02,820
So if you want to evaluate
the bony intervertebral

202
00:10:02,820 --> 00:10:05,390
foramen on x-ray, you need
to order oblique views.

203
00:10:05,390 --> 00:10:07,170
In the lumbar spine, you
would just get lateral.

204
00:10:14,120 --> 00:10:16,800
The disc is not like it
is in the lumbar spine.

205
00:10:16,800 --> 00:10:19,720
the annulus in the lumbar
spine is like this shell

206
00:10:19,720 --> 00:10:22,920
of spirally oriented
fibers all the way around

207
00:10:22,920 --> 00:10:25,720
360 degrees. It's not
like that in the C-spine.

208
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:30,060
The fibers are oriented
nearly vertically and it's

209
00:10:30,060 --> 00:10:34,560
really only in the anterior
half around the body. The

210
00:10:34,560 --> 00:10:37,080
back is pretty open except
for the posterior longitudinal

211
00:10:37,080 --> 00:10:41,200
ligament. The pulposus
becomes fibrotic much earlier

212
00:10:41,200 --> 00:10:44,500
in life normally than
the lumbar discs. So disc

213
00:10:44,500 --> 00:10:47,380
herniations aren't the norm
here. That's not to say that

214
00:10:47,380 --> 00:10:50,520
the disc can't protrude and
impinge on the nerve root.

215
00:10:50,520 --> 00:10:53,860
There's no doubt about that.
But as a cause for axial

216
00:10:53,860 --> 00:10:56,780
neck pain, it's less clear
how often that happens.

217
00:10:57,500 --> 00:11:00,520
It almost certainly
does. They've done

218
00:11:00,600 --> 00:11:03,800
assays of the pulposis and
found that it has inflammatory

219
00:11:03,800 --> 00:11:06,200
mediators. They've done
anatomical studies on

220
00:11:06,200 --> 00:11:08,760
the angulus and shown
that it's innervated. And

221
00:11:08,760 --> 00:11:11,080
they've done studies where
they've injected saline into

222
00:11:11,080 --> 00:11:13,580
the discs of normal subject
to high pressures and

223
00:11:13,580 --> 00:11:16,760
mapped out where the pain
was. But all of this amounts

224
00:11:16,760 --> 00:11:19,000
to basic science evidence
and the potential for

225
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:22,500
pain exists but you can't
make that leap clinically.

226
00:11:23,180 --> 00:11:26,700
Now that disc injection
technique is used diagnostically

227
00:11:26,700 --> 00:11:29,260
but it's technically
challenging to do right.

228
00:11:29,260 --> 00:11:31,720
Probably the best study
we have on this was done

229
00:11:31,720 --> 00:11:35,100
on patients with neck
pain, and it was found that

230
00:11:35,100 --> 00:11:39,460
the disc alone was a cause
in about 16% of patients.

231
00:11:39,600 --> 00:11:43,120
The disc and the facet
joint together, acting

232
00:11:43,120 --> 00:11:46,020
in a coordinated fashion
to create pain, about

233
00:11:46,020 --> 00:11:48,660
40%. But it was a small
sample, and it's hard

234
00:11:48,660 --> 00:11:50,740
to extrapolate that to
the general population.

235
00:11:50,890 --> 00:11:54,160
So the disc can be a source
of pain in the neck, but

236
00:11:54,160 --> 00:11:56,820
how often does that happen
is very unclear. here.

